/** \file virtualPDU.dox
 * \brief Virtual PDU configuration, INDI protocol, and telemetry documentation.
 * \ingroup virtualPDU_files
 */
/** \page page_module_virtualPDU virtualPDU
 * \ingroup virtualPDU
 *
 * \section virtualPDU_description Description
 * A virtual outlet maps to one named channel on another outlet-controller application.
 * Standard channel sections combine these outlets into one power control for a device.
 * The virtual PDU sends commands in configured order with fixed delays; it does not wait
 * for remote switches to change state. Its observed state comes only from incoming
 * channel state elements, never from targets or successful sends.
 *
 * \section virtualPDU_config Configuration
 * Start a configured instance with `virtualPDU -n vpdu0`. Use contiguous sections
 * `[outlet1]` through `[outletN]`, each containing `device` and `channel`. Numbers in
 * `outlet`/`outlets` are one-based; indices in `onOrder`/`offOrder` are zero-based.
 * Every order must be a permutation of the positions in that channel's outlet list.
 * `onDelays` and `offDelays` are milliseconds between commands; their first entry is
 * ignored. Omitted orders/delays retain the standard outlet-controller defaults.
 *
 * \include apps/virtualPDU/config/example.conf
 *
 * The normal `[power]` configuration of a consumer becomes:
 * \code{.ini}
 * [power]
 * device=vpdu0
 * channel=fwtelsim
 * \endcode
 * Use the virtual device/channel in the power GUI configuration to consolidate controls.
 * The GUI disables a channel's slider for Unk or any unrecognized observed state,
 * retaining its last displayed position. A recognized On, Off, or Int observation
 * restores control subject to the normal pending-command wait; targets and timeouts
 * cannot restore control while the observed state is unknown.
 * Physical controller channel configuration is unchanged. Deployment configuration and
 * actual instrument validation are performed by the user.
 *
 * Self-reference, duplicate endpoint aliases, and endpoints shared by virtual channels
 * are rejected. Shared endpoints are a known future use-case. Reject dependency cycles
 * and conflicting control across separate instances when designing deployment configs.
 * Configuration is loaded at startup; live reconfiguration is not supported.
 *
 * `[device] pollInterval` sets explicit source refresh spacing in seconds (default 5).
 * `staleTimeout` sets the maximum age of valid channel/FSM observations (default 15),
 * and must exceed pollInterval. A source must report Text `fsm.state=READY` and a valid
 * Text channel state before commands are accepted. A fresh Unk state is valid and permits
 * dispatch while keeping observed power unknown, matching the standard outlet interface. Target-only messages do not refresh
 * observed-state validity. Because dispatch uses the existing synchronous delay logic,
 * choose a stale limit longer than the longest sequence plus refresh/transport latency.
 *
 * Unavailable dependencies block only virtual channels that use them. The service itself
 * stays READY. Source loss/expiry makes affected virtual outlets unknown. A mid-sequence
 * failure stops later sends without rollback or automatic replay of already sent commands.
 * The refresh interval bounds detection delay; a distributed power operation is not atomic.
 *
 * \section virtualPDU_errors Configuration diagnostics
 * Startup rejects invalid configuration with a diagnostic identifying the failed condition
 * and its section, keyword, value, or required range. If two channels use the same virtual
 * outlet, the message names both channels and the one-based outlet number. A repeated outlet
 * within one channel is reported separately. Duplicate endpoint mappings identify both
 * `[outletN]` sections and the remote device/channel.
 *
 * Other diagnostics cover missing/gapped mappings, missing device/channel fields, invalid
 * refresh/expiry intervals, self-reference, reserved names, malformed numeric values,
 * outlet bounds, delay limits, order permutations, and mismatched order/delay entry counts.
 * Each order/delay array must have the same number of entries as that channel's outlet list.
 * These errors request shutdown; sharing outlets remains unsupported.
 *
 * \section virtualPDU_indi INDI properties
 * Each virtual channel is a read/write Text property with `state` and `target` elements.
 * Targets are On/Off; observed states are On, Off, Int, or Unk. All outlets must report
 * On for the aggregate to be On, and Off for it to be Off; differing states produce Int,
 * matching the standard outlet-controller aggregation and power-FSM interface.
 *
 * The standard `outlet`, `stateTimes`, `channelOutlets`, `channelOnDelays`, and
 * `channelOffDelays` properties are also exposed. channelOutlets retains the base's
 * internal zero-based numeric metadata. Delay metadata describes virtual dispatch delays,
 * not remote channel transition, nested sequence, or device boot times.
 *
 * \section virtualPDU_telemetry Telemetry
 * All four outlet-controller apps record telem_outlet snapshots on initial acquisition,
 * observed changes/invalidation, and periodic forcing. The variable-length signed-byte
 * vector uses -1 unknown, 0 off, 1 intermediate, and 2 on. first_outlet preserves physical
 * numbering (zero for the USB hub, one for the PDU/DCDU/virtual PDU).
 *
 * FITS accessors `states` and `first_outlet` use HIERARCH OUTLET STATES and OUTLET FIRST.
 * States are a comma-separated numeric string, including negative unknown values; the
 * binary payload retains compact signed bytes. Use the logged/controller configuration
 * to interpret outlet-to-channel identity.
 *
 * Tripp Lite additionally records telem_pdu frequency [Hz], voltage [V], current [A],
 * and valid. Only a complete successful electrical parse produces a valid sample.
 * Failed reads, deferred responses, and incomplete/malformed parses invalidate the
 * sample. Retained numbers in an invalid record must not be used as measurements.
 * Electrical FITS fields use state/sample selection and PDU FREQUENCY, PDU VOLTAGE,
 * PDU CURRENT, and PDU VALID; include valid alongside the values in header configuration.
 *
 * Standard `[telemeter]` options, including maxInterval, control recording intervals.
 * The legacy per-command outlet_state/outlet_channel_state event logs are preserved.
 */
